WEBVTT

NOTE Weekly operations sync. Speakers are support, ops and engineering.

1
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:09.800
<v Marco Reyes>Alright, weekly ops. Dana, you had numbers on the support queue.

2
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:17.600
<v Dana Whitfield>Yeah. Ticket volume was up about twelve percent last week, mostly
driven by one category.

3
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.400
<v Dana Whitfield>Refund status. People place a refund, nothing happens visibly, and
four days later they write in asking where their money is.

4
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:29.300
<v Marco Reyes>Is that a spike or is it just the usual level and we're noticing it more?

5
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.900
<v Dana Whitfield>It's the usual level honestly. It's just the biggest single bucket now
that shipping complaints have dropped off.

6
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:41.200
<v Priya Raman>The delay itself is mostly the provider. Once we hand a refund to
payments it can sit for days on their side.

7
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:46.000
<v Marco Reyes>So we can't make it faster, but the customer doesn't know anything is happening.

8
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:53.700
<v Marco Reyes>Can we just tell them? If a refund has been sitting for more than a few
days, automatically email the customer that it's still in progress.

9
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:58.100
<v Dana Whitfield>That would kill most of these tickets. They're not angry, they just
don't know anything.

10
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:05.300
<v Priya Raman>We'd need to know why it's delayed though. Right now we know a refund
was requested and we know when, and that's it.

11
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:11.400
<v Priya Raman>A provider hold, a manual review and a failed callback all look exactly
the same in our data.

12
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.800
<v Marco Reyes>Does it matter for a first version? Even "still processing" is better
than silence.

13
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:22.900
<v Priya Raman>Maybe. The other problem is we don't send customer email from the store
service at all. That's notifications-service.

14
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.700
<v Dana Whitfield>Which is Ravi's team, right? We'd need them to give us access.

15
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.500
<v Priya Raman>Right, comms owns it. We'd need credentials and probably a template approved.

16
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:38.400
<v Marco Reyes>Okay. Let's look at what it takes. Priya, can you scope what's involved
on the store side before next week?

17
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.600
<v Priya Raman>Sure, I'll take a look.

18
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:47.800
<v Dana Whitfield>One more thing while we're here, nobody has actually decided how many
days counts as "delayed". Support is guessing case by case.

19
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:52.100
<v Marco Reyes>Fair. That's a decision we need to make, not something to build around.

20
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:57.400
<v Marco Reyes>Last thing, the Q3 warehouse audit is done, no findings. Nothing needed there.

21
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.200
<v Dana Whitfield>And I'm out Thursday and Friday, Sam is covering the queue.

22
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.500
<v Marco Reyes>Noted. That's us, thanks everyone.
